  "pathDiscoveryStart": "My turn to play",
  "pathMultiplicationTip": "{table, select, 1{Multiplying by 1 changes nothing: 1 × 7 = 7.} 2{Multiplying by 2 means adding the number to itself: 2 × 6 = 6 + 6.} 3{The answers go up by 3 each time: 3, 6, 9, 12…} 4{Multiplying by 4 means doubling twice: 4 × 6 is double 12.} 5{The answers in the 5 times table always end in 0 or 5.} 6{6 times an even number ends with that same digit: 6 × 4 = 24.} 7{For 7 × 8, think 5, 6, 7, 8: 56 = 7 × 8.} 8{Multiplying by 8 means doubling three times: 8 × 3 = 24.} 9{The two digits of the answer always add up to 9: 9 × 4 = 36 and 3 + 6 = 9.} 10{Multiplying by 10 means adding a 0 on the right: 10 × 7 = 70.} 11{Up to 11 × 9, you write the digit twice: 11 × 4 = 44.} other{12 times a number is 10 times plus 2 times: 12 × 3 = 30 + 6.}}",
  "numbersUnitName": "{unit, select, count10{Count to 10} count20{Count to 20} count100{Count to 100} tensones{Tens and ones} compare{Compare} order{Put in order} beforeafter{Before and after} other{}}",
  "numbersUnitTip": "{unit, select, count10{Touch each mango as you count: one, two, three… The last number tells how many there are.} count20{First count the row of 10, then keep going: 11, 12, 13…} count100{A bundle is 10 sticks. Count the bundles in tens: 10, 20, 30…} tensones{The tens digit counts the bundles, the ones digit the loose sticks: 3 bundles and 4 sticks make 34.} compare{Look at the tens first: the number with more tens is greater. If the tens are the same, look at the ones.} order{On the line, numbers go from smallest to greatest. Find how much they go up each time.} beforeafter{The number after is 1 more; the number before is 1 less. After 39 comes 40!} other{}}",
  "divisionUnitName": "Divide by {number}",
  "divisionUnitTip": "{unit, select, 1{Dividing by 1 changes nothing: 7 ÷ 1 = 7.} 2{Dividing by 2 means sharing into two equal parts: 12 ÷ 2 = 6, half of 12.} 3{Dividing by 3 means making 3 equal parts: 12 ÷ 3 = 4, because 3 × 4 = 12.} 4{Dividing by 4 means halving twice: 20 ÷ 4 = 5, because half of 20 is 10 and half of 10 is 5.} 5{For 35 ÷ 5, count by 5s up to 35: 5, 10, 15, 20, 25, 30, 35. That is 7 jumps, so 35 ÷ 5 = 7.} 6{For 42 ÷ 6, look in the 6 times table: 6 × 7 = 42, so 42 ÷ 6 = 7.} 7{For 56 ÷ 7, think of 7 × 8 = 56: so 56 ÷ 7 = 8.} 8{Dividing by 8 means halving three times: for 48 ÷ 8, 48 → 24 → 12 → 6.} 9{In the 9 times table, the digits add up to 9 (36: 3 + 6 = 9). And 36 ÷ 9 = 4, because 9 × 4 = 36.} 10{Dividing by 10 means removing the 0 on the right: 70 ÷ 10 = 7.} 11{Up to 99 ÷ 11, the numbers in the 11 times table have two identical digits: 77 ÷ 11 = 7.} 12{For 36 ÷ 12, count by 12s: 12, 24, 36. That is 3 jumps, so 36 ÷ 12 = 3.} other{}}",
  "additionUnitName": "{unit, select, add5{Additions up to 5} sub5{Subtractions up to 5} add7{Additions up to 7} add9{Additions up to 9} toten{Complete to 10} sub7{Subtractions up to 7} sub9{Subtractions up to 9} subten{Subtractions from 10} add12{Additions up to 12} add14{Additions up to 14} add20{Additions up to 20} sub12{Subtractions up to 12} sub14{Subtractions up to 14} sub20{Subtractions up to 20} add100{Additions without carrying} sub100{Subtractions without borrowing} carry{Additions with carrying} borrow{Subtractions with borrowing} other{}}",
  "additionUnitTip": "{unit, select, add5{To add, move along the line: 2 + 3? Start at 2 and go up 3: 3, 4, 5!} sub5{To subtract is to take away. 5 − 2? Start at 5 and go back 2: 4, 3!} add7{To add, start from the bigger number and count up the smaller one. 2 + 5? Start at 5 and go up 2: 6, 7!} add9{Think of doubles: 4 + 4 = 8, so 4 + 5 = 9.} toten{Find what is missing to reach 10. 7 needs 3, because 7 + 3 = 10. Count on your fingers if you like!} sub7{To subtract is to take away. 7 − 3? Start at 7 and go back 3: 6, 5, 4. Or ask: 3 plus what makes 7?} sub9{For 9 − 4, find what to add to 4 to make 9: 4 + 5 = 9, so 9 − 4 = 5.} subten{For 10 − 3, think of the complement: 3 + 7 = 10, so 10 − 3 = 7.} add12{For 9 + 2, first go up to 10: 9 + 1 = 10. There is 1 left to add: 10 + 1 = 11.} add14{For 8 + 5, first go up to 10: 8 + 2 = 10. There are 3 left to add: 10 + 3 = 13.} add20{For 7 + 8, first go up to 10: 7 + 3 = 10. There are 5 left to add: 10 + 5 = 15.} sub12{For 12 − 4, first take away 2 to reach 10, then 2 more: 12 − 2 = 10, then 10 − 2 = 8.} sub14{For 13 − 6, first take away 3 to reach 10, then 3 more: 13 − 3 = 10, then 10 − 3 = 7.} sub20{For 15 − 7, first take away 5 to reach 10, then 2 more: 15 − 5 = 10, then 10 − 2 = 8.} add100{Add the ones, then the tens. For 23 + 14: 3 + 4 = 7 and 20 + 10 = 30, so 37.} sub100{Subtract the ones, then the tens. For 47 − 23: 7 − 3 = 4 and 40 − 20 = 20, so 24.} carry{When the ones make 10 or more, you carry one ten. For 27 + 15, 7 + 5 = 12: write 2 and carry 1 ten. 20 + 10 + 10 = 40, and 40 + 2 = 42.} borrow{If you cannot take away the ones, break a ten. For 42 − 17: 42 becomes 30 and 12. 12 − 7 = 5 and 30 − 10 = 20, so 25.} other{}}",
  "mathWordsUnitName": "{unit, select, add{Add} remove{Take away} compare{Compare} group{Group} share{Share} ask{The questions} other{}}",
  "mathWordsUnitTip": "{unit, select, add{When we add, we have more than before. Listen for “en plus”, “en tout”, “ensemble”: they say we put things together.} remove{When we take away, there is less left. The words “il reste”, “vendre”, “perdre” say the amount goes down.} compare{To compare, look at two amounts: one is bigger, the other smaller, or they are equal.} group{Packs of the same size are equal groups. The words “dans chaque” and “par paquets de” announce them.} share{To share is to make equal parts: everyone gets as much as the others.} ask{These words say what we look for (“combien”, “le total”) or when it happens (“au départ”, “à la fin”).} other{}}",
